    "long_instructions": "## Who decides what gets filtered out?\r\n<div style=\"background-color:#f3f0ff; border-radius:10px; padding:16px; margin:18px auto; max-width:85%; box-sizing:border-box;\"> <p style=\"font-size:14pt; margin-top:0; line-height:140%;\"><strong>Ethics question:</strong> If an algorithm filters out certain viewpoints “to keep people engaged,” is that helping users… or controlling them?</p> <p style=\"font-size:13pt; margin-bottom:0; line-height:140%;\"> <i>Consider who benefits from the filter rules: the user, the platform, advertisers, or someone else.</i> </p> </div> <details style=\"background-color:#ececec; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:8px;\"> <summary style=\"font-size:1.4em; font-weight:bold;\">💡 Check your thinking</summary> <div style=\"margin-top:12px; margin-left:14px; margin-right:14px; margin-bottom:10px; font-size:13pt; line-height:145%;\"> <p style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Filters aren’t neutral.</strong> Even when they’re “automatic,” humans choose goals and tradeoffs—like prioritizing engagement, safety, personalization, or profit.</p> <ul> <li style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Platform designers</strong> decide what the algorithm optimizes for.</li> <li style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Policy teams</strong> decide what content is allowed or removed.</li> <li style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Users</strong> sometimes choose filters—when the design makes them visible.</li> </ul> <p style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Transparency matters</strong> because it helps users understand what they’re not seeing and make informed decisions.</p> </div> </details>\r\n\r\n",
